<p><strong>A wellness center, massage studio or day spa in Nepal needs booking software that books the practitioner and the room together, sells treatment packages and memberships, and records every payment. A plain diary cannot stop two guests landing in the same room, or tell you what a package still owes.</strong></p>
<p>Wellness businesses sit between a spa and a clinic. Treatments are long, practitioners are named, rooms are limited and many guests prepay for a course. Software should reflect that.</p>
<h2>Practitioner and room, booked as one</h2>
<p>A massage is only available if a therapist and a room are both free for the whole treatment. Look for conflict-free scheduling that checks both at once, across branches if you have more than one. See <a href="/features/booking/">how Zennly books</a>.</p>
<h2>Packages, memberships and vouchers</h2>
<p>Guests often buy a course of sessions, a monthly membership or a gift. The system should track the remaining balance and record each visit against it. Read about <a href="/features/packages/">packages</a>, <a href="/features/memberships/">memberships</a> and <a href="/features/vouchers/">vouchers</a>.</p>
<h2>A clean daily close</h2>
<p>Cash, card and online payments should be logged as they happen, so closing the day is a report, not a reconciliation by hand. See <a href="/features/payment-tracking/">payment tracking</a> and <a href="/blog/daily-reconciliation/">what daily reconciliation should actually take</a>.</p>
<h2>Therapist pay</h2>
<p>Commission and attendance deductions are easier when bookings and attendance sit in one system. See <a href="/features/payroll/">payroll</a>.</p>
